# PCPP Level 3 Syllabus Alignment

When working on Level 3 course content, ensure all materials align with the official PCPP-32-101 exam syllabus (`Level_3.pdf`).

## PCPP Objective Coverage

All course content must explicitly reference PCPP objectives (1.1–5.2):

- **Section 1 (1.1–1.12)**: Advanced Object-Oriented Programming
- **Section 2 (2.1–2.3)**: Coding Conventions & Best Practices
- **Section 3 (3.1–3.3)**: GUI Programming
- **Section 4 (4.1–4.4)**: Network Programming
- **Section 5 (5.1–5.2)**: File Processing & Environment

## Code Examples

When creating code examples:

- Tag examples with PCPP objectives: `# PCPP 1.2: Magic methods`
- Ensure examples demonstrate the exact concepts listed in the syllabus
- Include edge cases and common mistakes mentioned in the syllabus

## Project Specifications

All mini projects and capstones must:

- List which PCPP objectives they cover
- Include acceptance criteria that verify objective coverage
- Provide extensions that deepen understanding of objectives

## Assessment Questions

Quiz and exam questions should:

- Map directly to PCPP objectives
- Use terminology from the official syllabus
- Test understanding at the depth required by the exam

## Documentation Standards

- Module content documents should reference PCPP objectives in lesson headers
- Project specs should include "PCPP Objectives" section
- Rubrics should track objective coverage

## Example

```python
# PCPP 1.2: Magic methods - Comparison methods
class Point:
    """Point class demonstrating __eq__ and __lt__ magic methods."""
    
    def __init__(self, x: int, y: int) -> None:
        self.x = x
        self.y = y
    
    def __eq__(self, other: object) -> bool:
        """PCPP 1.2: Comparison method for equality."""
        if not isinstance(other, Point):
            return NotImplemented
        return self.x == other.x and self.y == other.y
    
    def __lt__(self, other: 'Point') -> bool:
        """PCPP 1.2: Comparison method for less-than."""
        if not isinstance(other, Point):
            return NotImplemented
        return (self.x ** 2 + self.y ** 2) < (other.x ** 2 + other.y ** 2)
```

